  "notes": "inherent: The inherent vowel is typed 'a' inside a word and dropped at its end (घर ghar). Speech drops it inside words too (करत karat, करतानी kartani), and typists follow speech or the letters; the skeleton drops every a between two consonants so both give one key. letters: ऽ (avagraha) lengthens the vowel before it and is typed as nothing or as a (कऽ ka, हऽ ha); it is a final in the tables. Candrabindu is n or nothing (हँ han / ha, में men / me), anusvara n, or m before a labial. ज्ञ is gy (ज्ञान gyan). A nukta letter is its consonant plus U+093C after NFC, so its key is two code points; ड़ is r or d (बड़ bar / bad, लइका and लड़िका). skeleton: Aspirates fold to their plain letters, sh and chh/ch to s and c, z to j, f to p, w to v, q to k; aa, ee and oo to a, i and u; mb and mp to nb and np. A final a after a consonant is dropped, which covers the final inherent vowel typed or not."
